['Here is C code that generates three random integers larger than 1000, prints them out, and calculates their sums. It uses the rand() function to generate random numbers which are modulated by a number greater than 1000 to ensure they're above 1000:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL)); // Seed the random number generator with the current time

    long long a = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // Generate a random integer between 1000 and 10000
    long long b = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // Generate another random integer
    long long c = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // And a third one

    printf("a: %lld\n", a);
    printf("b: %lld\n", b);
    printf("c: %lld\n", c);

    long long sum = a + b + c; 
    printf("sum: %lld\n", sum);

    // If the sums are not expected to exceed the maximum limit of an int, you can use 'int' instead of 'long long'.
    // However, if they do, then 'long long' is required.
    long long sum2 = (long long)a + b + c; 
    printf("sum2: %lld\n", sum2);

    return 0;
}
```
This code makes use of the rand() function to generate random numbers and srand() to seed the number generator. Please note that this is not truly random as it's based on the time, so running this program multiple times in rapid succession will produce the same results. To get real randomness, you might want to use a more complex method of generating random numbers, such as using /dev/urandom or a library like OpenSSL's RAND_bytes().
']

['Here is an example of how you can do this in C using the `rand()` function to generate random numbers, and `abs()` to calculate the absolute difference:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Generate a random number between 10 and 1000
    int num1 = rand() % (1000 - 10 + 1) + 10;

    // Generate another random number between 10 and 1000
    int num2 = rand() % (1000 - 10 + 1) + 10;

    // Calculate the difference between the two numbers
    int diff = abs(num1 - num2);

    // Print the result
    printf("The difference between %d and %d is %d\n", num1, num2, diff);

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that `rand()` generates a pseudo-random number. If you want to generate different random numbers each time you run the program, you need to initialize it using `srand((unsigned int)time(NULL))` before generating the first random number. Also, be careful when including headers that use a lot of system resources or depend on platform specific features as they can cause undefined behavior if not used correctly.
']

