['Here's a simple implementation in C that uses `malloc` and `memset` for allocation and initialization, respectively. Note that we are using `calloc` to ensure that every byte is zeroed out, which may not be necessary if you only want to print the contents of the buffer (which might have garbage values).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <buffer_size>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int bufferSize = atoi(argv[1]);
    char *buffer = (char *)calloc(bufferSize, sizeof(char));

    if (buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for the buffer.\n");
        return 2;
    }

    // Print contents of the buffer
    printf("Contents of the buffer:\n%.*s\n", bufferSize, buffer);

    free(buffer);
    return 0;
}
```
In this code:
- We use `atoi` function to convert the command line argument from string to integer.
- If allocation fails (returns `NULL`), we print an error message and exit with a non-zero status code.
- After printing the contents of the buffer, we free the allocated memory using `free`.

Please note that it doesn't make sense to fill the buffer with any data because when the program starts, all memory is not initialized, so it contains garbage values which are printed by `printf`. If you want to print only zeros instead of garbage, replace `calloc` with `malloc`.
']

