    file = fopen("input.txt", "rb");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open input file.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Read the contents of the file into a buffer
    bytesRead = fread(buffer, sizeof(unsigned char), BUFSIZE, file);

    // Calculate checksum
    for (i = 0; i < bytesRead; ++i) {
        checksum += buffer[i];
    }

    // Close the input file
    fclose(file);

    if (checksum == 0) {
        printf("Buffer is empty.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Open an output file to save the number of bytes read and checksum
    FILE *outFile = fopen("output.txt", "w");
    if (outFile == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open output file.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Save the number of bytes read and checksum to a file
    fprintf(outFile, "Number of bytes read: %zu\nChecksum: %u", bytesRead, checksum);

    // Close output file
    fclose(outFile);

    return 0;
}
```
This code assumes that the input file is named `input.txt` and it's located in the same directory as your program. The output will be written to a file named `output.txt`, also in the same directory. If you need different behavior, you can adjust the filenames accordingly. Also note that this code does not handle errors properly, so for a robust solution, error checking should be added where appropriate.
']

['Here's an example of how you might implement this in C, using the standard library functions `fopen`, `fread`, `fwrite`, and `fclose` for file operations, and a simple checksum calculation based on summing all byte values. Please note that error checking is minimal here - in a real program, you would want to check return codes from each operation to ensure they were successful.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

int main() {
    FILE *inputFile = fopen("input.txt", "rb"); // Open the input file in binary mode
    if (inputFile == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open input file.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    unsigned char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
    size_t bytesRead = fread(buffer, sizeof(unsigned char), BUFFER_SIZE, inputFile); // Read from the file into the buffer
    fclose(inputFile); // Close the input file

    int checksum = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < bytesRead; i++) {
        checksum += buffer[i]; // Add up all byte values to calculate the checksum
    }

    FILE *outputFile = fopen("output.txt", "wb"); // Open the output file in binary mode
    if (outputFile == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open output file.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    fwrite(&bytesRead, sizeof(size_t), 1, outputFile); // Write number of bytes read
    fwrite(&checksum, sizeof(int), 1, outputFile); // Write checksum
    fclose(outputFile); // Close the output file

    return 0;
}
```
This program assumes that `input.txt` and `output.txt` are in the same directory as your compiled executable. You should replace these with your actual filenames. The checksum is an integer value, so it will be platform-dependent if you're moving this code between different platforms (for example, between a 32-bit and a 64-bit system).
']

