You should take note that `open()` and `write()` are system calls which interact directly with the operating system. If these calls fail for any reason (like file permissions issue, disk full etc.), they will return a negative value. We handle this scenario by checking if `fd` is less than 0 and calling `perror("open")` to print out an error message if this happens.

Also note that we're using the correct system call for write (`write()`), as well as correctly closing our file descriptor with `close(fd)`. Failure to do so could lead to resource leaks in your program, as open files are a limited resource on most systems. 

The permissions argument passed into `open()` is octal (base-8) for the mode of the file. Here, the number `0700` means that the owner will have read, write and execute permissions(4+2+1=7 in decimal). The group and others won't have any permissions (0).
